**Solution Explanation**

For one query `x , y , z`

```
answer = Σ a[i]          ( x ≤ i ≤ y  and  a[i] > z )
```

`N , Q` can be large, therefore a separate scan of the interval for every
query is too slow (`O(N·Q)`).

--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### 1.   Offline processing  

All queries are known before we start answering them, so we may reorder them.
Consider the following order

```
queries sorted by decreasing z
array elements sorted by decreasing value
```

While we walk through the queries we keep a data structure that already
contains **exactly those array positions whose value is larger than the
current `z`**.  
If the next query has a smaller `z`, more elements become “large enough”,
they are inserted into the structure, never removed.

For a query we only have to know the sum of the inserted values inside
`[x , y]`.  
A Fenwick tree (Binary Indexed Tree) supports

* `add(pos , value)` – insert one element
* `sum(l , r)`      – sum on an interval  

both in `O(log N)` time.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### 2.   Algorithm
```
read N , Q
read array a[1 … N]

elements = (a[i] , i) for i = 1 … N
sort elements decreasing by a[i]

queries = (x , y , z , id) for each query, id = its original order
sort queries decreasing by z

Fenwick tree FT of size N, initially all zeros
ptr = 0                                 // points into elements

for each query q in sorted order
        while ptr < N  and  elements[ptr].value > q.z
                FT.add( elements[ptr].index , elements[ptr].value )
                ++ptr
        ans[q.id] = FT.sum( q.x , q.y )   // sum on interval [x , y]

output ans[0] … ans[Q‑1] in the original order
```

--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### 3.   Correctness Proof  

We prove that the algorithm prints the required answer for every query.

---

##### Lemma 1  
When a query `q = (x , y , z)` is processed, the Fenwick tree contains
exactly the values `a[i]` with `a[i] > z` and `i` in `{1 … N}`.

**Proof.**  
Elements are processed in decreasing order of their value.
`ptr` is increased only while `elements[ptr].value > q.z`.  
Consequently after the `while` loop finishes

```
   for every i < ptr : a[i] > q.z   (inserted)
   for every i ≥ ptr : a[i] ≤ q.z   (not inserted)
```

No element is ever removed, therefore the tree contains precisely the
positions whose value is larger than the current `z`. ∎



##### Lemma 2  
For a processed query `q = (x , y , z)` the value returned by  
`FT.sum(x , y)` equals  

```
Σ a[i]   ( x ≤ i ≤ y  and  a[i] > z )
```

**Proof.**  
By Lemma&nbsp;1 the Fenwick tree stores *all* and *only* those positions
`i` with `a[i] > z`.  
`FT.sum(x , y)` adds the stored values whose indices lie in `[x , y]`,
hence it adds exactly the required `a[i]`. ∎



##### Lemma 3  
`ans[q.id]` stored by the algorithm equals the correct answer for query
`q`.

**Proof.**  
Directly from Lemma&nbsp;2, because the algorithm writes the value
`FT.sum(x , y)` into `ans[q.id]`. ∎



##### Theorem  
For every input query the algorithm outputs the sum of the elements in
the given interval that are larger than `z`.

**Proof.**  
All queries are processed once, each time Lemma&nbsp;3 guarantees that the
stored answer is correct.
Finally the answers are printed in the original order, therefore every
output line is the correct answer for the corresponding query. ∎



--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### 3.   Complexity Analysis  

*Sorting*  

* `elements` : `O(N log N)`
* `queries`  : `O(Q log Q)`

*Processing*  

Each array element is inserted **once** → `N` Fenwick updates.  
Each query performs one interval sum → `Q` Fenwick queries.  

Both operations are `O(log N)`, therefore

```
total time   :  O( (N + Q) · log N )
memory usage :  O(N + Q)          (array, queries, Fenwick tree, answers)
```

All sums fit into 64‑bit signed integer (`long long`).

--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### 4.   Reference Implementation  (GNU‑C++17)

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

struct Fenwick {
    int n;
    vector<long long> bit;          // 1‑based
    Fenwick(int n = 0) { init(n); }
    void init(int n_) {
        n = n_;
        bit.assign(n + 1, 0);
    }
    // add value v at position idx
    void add(int idx, long long v) {
        for (; idx <= n; idx += idx & -idx) bit[idx] += v;
    }
    // prefix sum [1 .. idx]
    long long sumPrefix(int idx) const {
        long long s = 0;
        for (; idx > 0; idx -= idx & -idx) s += bit[idx];
        return s;
    }
    // sum on interval [l , r]
    long long rangeSum(int l, int r) const {
        if (l > r) return 0;
        return sumPrefix(r) - sumPrefix(l - 1);
    }
};

struct Query {
    int l, r;
    long long z;
    int id;
};

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int N, Q;
    if (!(cin >> N >> Q)) return 0;
    vector<long long> a(N + 1);
    for (int i = 1; i <= N; ++i) cin >> a[i];

    // array elements (value, position)
    vector<pair<long long,int>> elem;
    elem.reserve(N);
    for (int i = 1; i <= N; ++i) elem.emplace_back(a[i], i);
    sort(elem.begin(), elem.end(),
         [](const auto& p1, const auto& p2){ return p1.first > p2.first; });

    // read queries
    vector<Query> qs(Q);
    for (int i = 0; i < Q; ++i) {
        int x, y;
        long long z;
        cin >> x >> y >> z;
        qs[i] = {x, y, z, i};
    }
    sort(qs.begin(), qs.end(),
         [](const Query& q1, const Query& q2){ return q1.z > q2.z; });

    Fenwick ft(N);
    vector<long long> ans(Q);
    int ptr = 0;                     // next element to insert

    for (const auto& q : qs) {
        while (ptr < N && elem[ptr].first > q.z) {
            ft.add(elem[ptr].second, elem[ptr].first);
            ++ptr;
        }
        ans[q.id] = ft.rangeSum(q.l, q.r);
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < Q; ++i) cout << ans[i] << '\n';
    return 0;
}
```

The program follows exactly the algorithm proven correct above
and conforms to the GNU++17 compiler.

