**Solution Explanation**

For a string `S` of length `N`

* all substrings =  

```
N + (N‑1) + … + 1 = N·(N+1)/2
```

If we write all suffixes of `S` in **lexicographic order**  

```
suffix[0] < suffix[1] < … < suffix[N‑1]
```

two consecutive suffixes share a common prefix.
All substrings that start inside this common prefix are counted twice
in the formula `N·(N+1)/2`.  
The length of the common prefix of `suffix[i]` and `suffix[i‑1]`
is exactly the **LCP** (Longest Common Prefix) of the two suffixes.

Therefore  

```
number of distinct substrings =
        total substrings – Σ LCP(i)   (i = 1 … N‑1)
```

So we only have to build

* the **suffix array** – the order of all suffixes,
* the **LCP array** – the length of the longest common prefix of two
  consecutive suffixes in that order.

Both can be built in `O(N log N)` (suffix array) and `O(N)` (LCP).

--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### 1.   Suffix array – doubling algorithm  

```
k = 1,2,4,8,… while k < N
    sort suffixes by the pair (rank[i] , rank[i+k])
    recompute new ranks
```

* `rank[i]` – rank of the suffix that starts at position `i`
  using the first `k` characters.
* For `k = 1` the rank is simply the character code.
* Sorting is done with `std::sort` and a comparator that uses the two
  integer ranks – `O(N log N)` per iteration.
* After at most `⌈log₂ N⌉` iterations the ranks become unique,
  i.e. the suffix array is finished.

The algorithm needs only three integer arrays of size `N`
(`sa`, `rank`, `tmp`) → `O(N)` memory.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### 2.   LCP array – Kasai algorithm  

```
pos[sa[i]] = i                // position of each suffix in SA
h = 0
for i = 0 … N‑1
        if pos[i] == 0   continue          // first suffix has no previous
        j = sa[pos[i]-1]                    // previous suffix in SA
        while i+h < N && j+h < N && S[i+h]==S[j+h]   h++
        lcp[pos[i]-1] = h
        if h > 0   h--
```

Runs in linear time, uses only the already built suffix array.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### 3.   Answer  

```
total = N·(N+1)/2                // 64‑bit integer
sumLCP = Σ lcp[i]                // i = 0 … N‑2
answer = total - sumLCP
```

--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### 4.   Correctness Proof  

We prove that the algorithm outputs the exact number of distinct
substrings.

---

##### Lemma 1  
For every `i (1 ≤ i < N)` the value `lcp[i-1]` computed by Kasai
equals the length of the longest common prefix of the suffixes
`suffix[sa[i-1]]` and `suffix[sa[i]]`.

**Proof.**  
Kasai’s algorithm is a classic linear‑time proof.
`pos` tells the rank of each suffix in the suffix array.
When processing position `i` we compare the suffix starting at `i`
with the suffix that is immediately before it in the suffix array,
exactly the definition of `LCP`.  
The variable `h` holds the length of the already known common prefix
for the previous position, therefore the while‑loop extends it only
as far as the characters still match. ∎



##### Lemma 2  
`Σ_{i=1}^{N-1} LCP(i)` (the sum of all LCP values) is the number of
substrings that are counted more than once in the total
`N·(N+1)/2`.

**Proof.**  
Consider the lexicographically ordered suffixes.
All substrings are prefixes of some suffix.
When we walk through the suffix array, the first suffix contributes all
its prefixes – none of them appeared before.
For the second suffix, its first `LCP(1)` prefixes are already present
as prefixes of the first suffix, therefore they are duplicates.
The same holds for every following suffix: its first `LCP(i)` prefixes
have already been seen as prefixes of the previous suffix.
No other duplicates exist, because any two different suffixes have a
different first character after their common prefix.
Hence the total number of duplicated substrings equals the sum of all
LCP values. ∎



##### Lemma 3  
`answer = N·(N+1)/2 – Σ LCP(i)` equals the number of distinct substrings
of `S`.

**Proof.**  
`N·(N+1)/2` counts *all* substrings (with repetitions).  
By Lemma&nbsp;2 the sum of LCPs counts exactly the repetitions.
Subtracting removes every duplicated occurrence once, leaving each
different substring exactly once. ∎



##### Theorem  
For each test case the algorithm prints the exact number of distinct
substrings of the given string.

**Proof.**  

1. The doubling algorithm produces a correct suffix array
   (standard result of the algorithm).
2. By Lemma&nbsp;1 the Kasai algorithm computes the correct LCP array.
3. By Lemma&nbsp;3 the formula using total substrings and the sum of the
   LCP array yields the exact number of distinct substrings.
4. The program outputs this value, therefore the answer is correct. ∎



--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### 5.   Complexity Analysis  

For a string of length `N`

* building the suffix array: `O(N log N)` time, `O(N)` memory  
  (each of `⌈log₂ N⌉` iterations sorts `N` integers)
* building the LCP array: `O(N)` time, `O(N)` memory
* final computation: `O(N)` time, `O(1)` extra memory

Overall per test case  

```
Time   : O(N log N)      ( ≤ 5·10⁴·log₂5·10⁴  ≈ 8·10⁵ operations )
Memory : O(N)            ( ≤ 5·10⁴ integers ≈ 0.6 MiB )
```

Both satisfy the limits easily.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### 6.   Reference Implementation  (GNU‑C++17)

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

using ll = long long;

/* ---------- suffix array (doubling) ---------- */
vector<int> buildSA(const string& s) {
    int n = (int)s.size();
    vector<int> sa(n), rank(n), tmp(n);
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        sa[i] = i;
        rank[i] = s[i];                 // initial rank = character code
    }

    for (int k = 1; k < n; k <<= 1) {
        auto cmp = [&](int i, int j) {
            if (rank[i] != rank[j]) return rank[i] < rank[j];
            int ri = (i + k < n) ? rank[i + k] : -1;
            int rj = (j + k < n) ? rank[j + k] : -1;
            return ri < rj;
        };
        sort(sa.begin(), sa.end(), cmp);

        tmp[sa[0]] = 0;
        for (int i = 1; i < n; ++i)
            tmp[sa[i]] = tmp[sa[i - 1]] + (cmp(sa[i - 1], sa[i]) ? 1 : 0);
        rank.swap(tmp);
        if (rank[sa[n - 1]] == n - 1) break;   // all ranks are distinct
    }
    return sa;
}

/* ---------- LCP array (Kasai) ---------- */
vector<int> buildLCP(const string& s, const vector<int>& sa) {
    int n = (int)s.size();
    vector<int> rank(n);
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) rank[sa[i]] = i;

    vector<int> lcp(n - 1);
    int h = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        int r = rank[i];
        if (r == 0) continue;               // first suffix has no previous
        int j = sa[r - 1];
        while (i + h < n && j + h < n && s[i + h] == s[j + h]) ++h;
        lcp[r - 1] = h;
        if (h) --h;
    }
    return lcp;
}

/* ---------- main ---------- */
int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);
    int T;
    if (!(cin >> T)) return 0;
    while (T--) {
        int N;
        string S;
        cin >> N >> S;
        vector<int> sa = buildSA(S);
        vector<int> lcp = buildLCP(S, sa);
        ll total = 1LL * N * (N + 1) / 2;
        ll sumLCP = 0;
        for (int x : lcp) sumLCP += x;
        cout << total - sumLCP << '\n';
    }
    return 0;
}
```

The program follows exactly the algorithm proven correct above and
conforms to the GNU++17 compiler.

