Below is a quick‑start guide that works for a **plain‑HTML/JavaScript web page**.  
If you are building a native Android or iOS app the same concepts apply – you just use the platform‑specific SDK instead of the JavaScript API (see the “Native‑app notes” at the end).

---

## 1️⃣  Get a Google Maps API key  

1. Open the **Google Cloud Console** → **APIs & Services → Library**.  
2. Enable **Maps JavaScript API** (and **Geolocation API** if you want server‑side location look‑ups).  
3. Go to **APIs & Services → Credentials** → **Create credentials → API key**.  
4. (Strongly recommended) Restrict the key to:
   * **Application restrictions** → *HTTP referrers* (your domain)  
   * **API restrictions** → *Maps JavaScript API* (and any others you’ll use)  

> **Billing** must be enabled on the project; the free tier gives you $200 of usage each month, which is enough for most small apps.

---

## 2️⃣  Basic HTML skeleton

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>My Map App</title>
  <style>
    /* Full‑screen map */
    #map { height: 100vh; width: 100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>

<div id="map"></div>

<!-- Load the Maps JavaScript API (replace YOUR_API_KEY) -->
<script
