Below is a practical “tool‑kit” you can stitch together to get everything you asked for – metadata, cover art, a short‑playable stream, and related videos – without having to rely on a single monolithic service.

| What you need | Best‑fit public API | What it gives you | How to combine it |
|---------------|--------------------|-------------------|-------------------|
| **Core music metadata (artist, album, track, release dates, MBIDs, etc.)** | **MusicBrainz** (https://musicbrainz.org/doc/Development/JSON_Web_Service) | • Accurate, community‑curated discography <br>• Unique MusicBrainz IDs (MBIDs) for every entity <br>• Relationships to other services (Spotify, YouTube, etc.) | Use the MBID as the canonical key in your own index. All downstream calls (cover art, streaming, videos) can be linked back to this ID. |
| **Album / release artwork** | **Cover Art Archive** (https://coverartarchive.org/) – a direct companion to MusicBrainz | • High‑resolution front‑cover images (and back/inner art when available) <br>• JSON endpoint: `https://coverartarchive.org/release/{mbid}` | Pull the `image` URL from the JSON response and cache it locally (or proxy it) for fast UI rendering. |
| **Playable preview / streaming URL** | **Spotify Web API** (https://developer.spotify.com/documentation/web-api/) **or** **Apple iTunes Search API** (https://developer.apple.com/library/archive/documentation/AudioVideo/Conceptual/iTuneSearchAPI/index.html) | • 30‑second preview MP3 (`preview_url`) for most tracks (Spotify) <br>• 30‑second preview MP4 (`previewUrl`) for many tracks (iTunes) <br>• Full‑track streaming URLs (requires user auth & a Spotify Premium account) | • First try Spotify – it has the richest catalog and returns the preview URL in the same track object you already get from MusicBrainz relationships. <br>• Fall back to iTunes if Spotify has no preview. <br>• Store the preview URL together with the MBID in your search index. |
| **Related videos (official music videos, live performances, lyric videos, etc.)** | **YouTube Data API v3** (https://developers.google.com/youtube/v3) | • Search results limited to official channels (use `channelId` filters for Vevo, official artist channels) <br>• Video IDs, thumbnails, durations, view counts | • Build a query like `"{artist name} {track title}"` and optionally add `official` or `lyric` keywords. <br>• Use the `videoId` to embed the player or to link out. <br>• Cache the top‑N results (e.g., first 3) per track in your DB for fast retrieval. |
| **Optional enrichment (tags, similar artists, user‑generated scrobbles)** | **Last.fm API** (https://www.last.fm/api) | • Genre tags, similar‑artist lists, listener counts <br>• Album and track “wiki” summaries | • Use the MusicBrainz MBID → Last.fm `artist.getInfo` / `track.getInfo` endpoints to pull tags and a short description. <br>• Show tags alongside the cover art for a richer UI. |

### How the pieces fit together in a typical search flow

1. **User types a query** → your backend sends the text to **MusicBrainz** (`/ws/2/artist`, `/ws/2/release`, `/ws/2/recording`) and receives a list of matches with MBIDs.  
2. **Pick the best match** (e.g., highest score, exact name, or user‑selected). Store the MBID as the canonical identifier.  
3. **Fetch cover art** from **Cover Art Archive** using the release MBID (`/release/{mbid}`).  
4. **Get a playable preview**:  
   * Call **Spotify** `GET /v1/tracks/{spotify_id}` (you can resolve the Spotify ID from the MusicBrainz relationship `url-relation` of type “streaming”).  
   * If `preview_url` is `null`, fall back to **iTunes** (`search?term={artist}+{track}&entity=song`).  
5. **Pull related videos**:  
   * Build a YouTube search query `"artist track official"` and request the first 3 results (`search.list` with `type=video`).  
   * Optionally filter by known official channel IDs (Vevo, artist’s verified channel).  
6. **Enrich with tags** (optional): Call **Last.fm** `track.getInfo` using the artist & track name (or MBID) to retrieve genre tags and a short description.  
7. **Cache** all of the above (metadata, cover‑art URL, preview URL, video IDs, tags) in your own datastore keyed by the MusicBrainz MBID. This makes subsequent searches instantaneous and lets you apply your own ranking / personalization logic.  

### Quick “starter‑code” snippets (Node.js / fetch)

```js
// 1️⃣ MusicBrainz search
async function searchMusicBrainz(q) {
  const resp = await fetch(
    `https://musicbrainz.org/ws/2/recording?query=${encodeURIComponent(q)}&fmt=json`
  );
  const data = await resp.json();
  return data.recordings; // each has id (MBID), title, artist‑credit, etc.
}

// 2️⃣ Cover art
async function getCoverArt(releaseMbid) {
  const resp = await fetch(`https://coverartarchive.org/release/${releaseMbid}`);
  if (!resp.ok) return null;
  const data = await resp.json();
  return data.images[0].image; // front cover URL
}

// 3️⃣ Spotify preview
async function getSpotifyPreview(spotifyTrackId, token) {
  const resp = await fetch(
    `https://api.spotify.com/v1/tracks/${spotifyTrackId}`,
    { headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${token}` } }
  );
  const data = await resp.json();
  return data.preview_url; // may be null
}

// 4️⃣ YouTube videos
async function getYouTubeVideos(query, apiKey) {
  const resp = await fetch(
    `https://www.googleapis.com/youtube/v3/search?part=snippet&type=video&maxResults=3&q=${encodeURIComponent(query)}&key=${apiKey}`
  );
  const data = await resp.json();
  return data.items.map(v => ({
    videoId: v.id.videoId,
    title: v.snippet.title,
    thumb: v.snippet.thumbnails.medium.url
  }));
}
```

### Things to keep in mind

| Issue | Mitigation |
|-------|------------|
| **Rate limits** – each API has its own quota (MusicBrainz: 1 req/s per IP; Spotify: 10 req/s per token; YouTube: 10 000 units/day). | Use a small server‑side cache (e.g., Redis) keyed by MBID. Batch requests where possible (Spotify’s “Get Several Tracks” endpoint). |
| **Missing previews** – not every track has a 30‑sec preview on Spotify or iTunes. | Show a “Play on Spotify/Apple Music” button that redirects to the full‑track page (you can get the external URL from the same API). |
| **YouTube copyright** – some videos may be blocked in certain regions. | Prefer official Vevo/artist channels; fallback to a generic “search on YouTube” link if the embed fails. |
| **Data consistency** – MusicBrainz IDs are stable, but external IDs (Spotify, iTunes) can change. | Store the external IDs alongside the MBID and refresh them periodically (e.g., nightly job). |
| **User authentication** – full‑track streaming via Spotify requires a user token. | For a pure “search‑engine” you can stay with the 30‑sec preview (no auth needed). If you later add “play full track”, implement OAuth 2.0 flow for Spotify. |

---

#### TL;DR – The minimal stack

1. **MusicBrainz** – core metadata & canonical IDs.  
2. **Cover Art Archive** – album art (front cover).  
3. **Spotify Web API** (or iTunes Search API) – 30‑sec preview URLs.  
4. **YouTube Data API** – related music‑video links.  

Add **Last.fm** for tags/genres if you want richer browsing. With these four services you can build a fast, reliable music‑search experience that returns everything a user expects: “Who sang this? What does the album look like? Can I listen to a snippet? And where’s the video?”

