| **$or, $and, $nor, $not, $exists, $type, $size, $elemMatch, $all, $slice, $mod** | All are demonstrated in the *Advanced Search* route. |

> **Why the “big‑list” of helpers is in one route**  
> In a real project you would split them into separate controller files (e.g. `user.controller.js`, `user.service.js`). The example keeps everything together so you can see **exactly where** each Mongoose helper is used.

---

## 7️⃣ `src/app.js` – Wire everything together

```js
// src/app.js
import express from 'express';
import bodyParser from 'body-parser';
import dotenv from 'dotenv';
import { connectDB } from './config/db.js';
import userRoutes from './routes/userRoutes.js';

dotenv.config();

const app = express();
const PORT = process.env.PORT || 3000;

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   Middleware
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
app.use(bodyParser.json());          // parses application/json
app.use(bodyParser.urlencoded({ extended: true })); // optional for form‑data

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   Routes
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
app.use('/api/users', userRoutes);

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   Global error fallback (optional)
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
app.use((err, _req, res, _next) => {
  console.error('Unhandled error:', err);
  res.status(500).json({ error: 'Something went wrong' });
});

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   Start the server
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
const start = async () => {
  await connectDB();
  app.listen(PORT, () => console.log(`🚀 Server listening on ${PORT}`));
};

start();
```

---

## 8️⃣ `.env` (example)

```
MONGODB_URI=mongodb://localhost:27017/myapp
PORT=4000
BCRYPT_SALT_ROUNDS=12
```

