Below is a **complete, self‑contained HTML file** that does exactly what you asked for:

* A small list of quotes lives in a JavaScript array.  
* When the page loads, a random quote is chosen and inserted into the page.  
* The markup is minimal, but you can easily add your own styling or expand the list.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <title>Random Quote</title>
    <style>
        body {
            font-family:  Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;
            background: #f0f4f8;
            color: #333;
            display: flex;
            align-items: center;
            justify-content: center;
            height: 100vh;
            margin: 0;
        }
        #quote-box {
            max-width: 600px;
            padding: 2rem;
            background: #fff;
            border-radius: 8px;
            box-shadow: 0 4px 12px rgba(0,0,0,.1);
            text-align: center;
        }
        #quote {
            font-size: 1.4rem;
            line-height: 1.5;
            margin-bottom: 1rem;
        }
        #author {
            font-size: 1rem;
            color: #555;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>

<div id="quote-box">
    <p id="quote">Loading…</p>
    <p id="author"></p>
</div>

<script>
    // 1️⃣  Your list of quotes (feel free to add more!)
    const quotes = [
        { text: "The only way to do great work is to love what you do.", author: "Steve Jobs" },
        { text: "Life is what happens when you're busy making other plans.", author: "John Lennon" },
        { text: "You miss 100% of the shots you don’t take.", author: "Wayne Gretzky" },
        { text: "The purpose of our lives is to be happy.", author: "Dalai Lama" },
        { text: "In the middle of difficulty lies opportunity.", author: "Albert Einstein" }
    ];

    // 2️⃣  Pick a random quote
    function getRandomQuote() {
        const idx = Math.floor(Math.random() * quotes.length);
        return quotes[idx];
    }

    // 3️⃣  Insert the quote into the page
    function displayQuote() {
        const { text, author } = getRandomQuote();
        document.getElementById('quote').textContent   = `"${text}"`;
        document.getElementById('author').textContent = `— ${author}`;
    }

    // Run as soon as the DOM is ready
    document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', displayQuote);
</script>

</body>
</html>
```

### How it works

| Step | What happens |
|------|--------------|
| **1️⃣** | An array `quotes` holds objects with `text` (the quote) and `author`. Add, remove, or edit entries here. |
| **2️⃣** | `getRandomQuote()` computes a random index (`Math.random() * quotes.length`) and returns the corresponding object. |
| **3️⃣** | `displayQuote()` extracts the `text` and `author` from the random object and writes them into the `<p>` elements with IDs `quote` and `author`. |
| **DOMContentLoaded** | The script waits until the HTML is parsed, then calls `displayQuote()` so the quote appears immediately on page load. |

### Customising

* **Add more quotes** – just push more objects into the `quotes` array.
* **Styling** – modify the CSS inside the `<style>` block to match your design.
* **Refresh button** – if you want a button that shows a new quote without reloading the page, add:

```html
<button id="new-quote">New Quote</button>
```

and then:

```js
document.getElementById('new-quote').addEventListener('click', displayQuote);
```

That’s it! Save the file as `index.html`, open it in any browser, and you’ll see a different quote each time the page loads (or each time you click the button, if you add one). Enjoy!

