twittercamp-1:
Below is a **complete, beginner‑friendly, step‑by‑step guide** for building a tiny web app that:

* lets a user type something,
* shows that text on the screen when a button is pressed,
* works on desktop **and** mobile,
* keeps HTML, CSS and JavaScript in separate files (the cleanest way to start).

---

## 1️⃣  Project set‑up

1. **Create a folder** for the project – e.g. `simple‑echo‑app`.
2. Inside that folder create three files:

```
simple-echo-app/
│
├─ index.html
├─ style.css
└─ script.js
```

> **Tip:** If you use VS Code, open the folder and press **Ctrl+Shift+`** (or *Terminal → New Terminal*) – you’ll have a live preview with the *Live Server* extension later.

---

## 2️⃣  HTML – the page skeleton

Open **`index.html`** and paste the following:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <!-- 1️⃣ Essential meta tags -->
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
  <title>Echo App – type & see</title>

  <!-- 2️⃣ Link to our CSS -->
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css">
</head>
<body>

  <!-- 3️⃣ Main container (centered, responsive) -->
  <main class="app">

    <h1>Echo App</h1>

    <!-- 4️⃣ Form – semantic, accessible -->
    <form id="echoForm" autocomplete="off">
      <label for="userInput">What do you want to say?</label>
      <input type="text"
             id="userInput"
             placeholder="Type something…"
             required>
      <button type="submit">Show it!</button>
    </form>

    <!-- 5️⃣ Output area – will be filled by JS -->
    <section id="output" aria-live="polite" role="status"></section>

  </main>

  <!-- 6️⃣ Link to our JavaScript (bottom of body = best practice) -->
  <script src="script.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

### What’s happening?

| Part | Why it matters |
|------|----------------|
| `meta viewport` | Makes the page scale correctly on phones. |
| `<main class="app">` | Gives us a single wrapper we can style (center, max‑width, padding). |
| `<form>` + `<label>` | Accessible – screen readers know what the input is for. |
| `required` on the `<input>` | Prevents empty submissions without extra JS. |
| `aria-live="polite"` on the output | Announces the new text to assistive tech. |
| JS placed **after** the HTML | Guarantees the DOM exists when the script runs. |

---

## 3️⃣  CSS – make it look good **and** responsive

Open **`style.css`** and paste:

```css
/* -------------------------------------------------
   1️⃣  Reset & basic box‑sizing (helps layout)
   ------------------------------------------------- */
*,
*::before,
*::after {
  box-sizing: border-box;
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
}

/* -------------------------------------------------
   2️⃣  Root font size – use rem for scalability
   ------------------------------------------------- */
html {
  font-size: 100%;               /* 1rem = 16px (default) */
}

/* -------------------------------------------------
   3️⃣  Body – background & default font
   ------------------------------------------------- */
body {
  min-height: 100vh;             /* full height on mobile */
  font-family: system-ui, -apple-system,
               BlinkMacSystemFont, "Segoe UI",
               Roboto, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif;
  line-height: 1.5;
  background: #f5f7fa;
  color: #333;
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
}

/* -------------------------------------------------
   4️⃣  .app – the central card
   ------------------------------------------------- */
.app {
  background: #fff;
  padding: 2rem;
  border-radius: 0.75rem;
  box-shadow: 0 4px 12px rgba(0,0,0,.08);
  max-width: 420px;              /* never wider than this */
  width: 90%;                    /* but shrink on tiny screens */
}

/* -------------------------------------------------
   5️⃣  Typography
   ------------------------------------------------- */
h1 {
  font-size: 1.75rem;
  margin-bottom: 1rem;
  text-align: center;
}

/* -------------------------------------------------
   6️⃣  Form layout – Flexbox column
   ------------------------------------------------- */
form {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  gap: 0.75rem;                  /* space between elements */
}

/* -------------------------------------------------
   7️⃣  Input & button styling
   ------------------------------------------------- */
input[type="text"] {
  padding: 0.75rem 1rem;
  font-size: 1rem;
  border: 2px solid #ddd;
  border-radius: 0.5rem;
  transition: border-color .2s;
}
input[type="text"]:focus {
  outline: none;
  border-color: #5c9ded;
}

/* Button – primary style */
button {
  padding: 0.75rem 1rem;
  font-size: 1rem;
  background: #5c9ded;
  color: #fff;
  border: none;
  border-radius: 0.5rem;
  cursor: pointer;
  transition: background .2s;
}
button:hover,
button:focus {
  background: #4a8ad4;
}

/* -------------------------------------------------
   8️⃣  Output area
   ------------------------------------------------- */
#output {
  margin-top: 1.5rem;
  padding: 1rem;
  min-height: 2rem;               /* reserve space before first output */
  background: #f0f8ff;
  border-left: 4px solid #5c9ded;
  border-radius: 0.25rem;
  font-size: 1.125rem;
  word-break: break-word;         /* long words wrap on small screens */
}

/* -------------------------------------------------
   9️⃣  Mobile‑first media query (optional tweaks)
   ------------------------------------------------- */
@media (max-width: 340px) {
  .app {
    padding: 1.5rem;
  }
  h1 {
    font-size: 1.5rem;
  }
}
```

### Why this CSS is “responsive”

* **`width: 90%` + `max-width: 420px`** → the card fills most of the screen on phones but never stretches too wide on a desktop.
* **Flexbox column + `gap`** → the form elements automatically stack with consistent spacing, no extra margins needed.
* **Relative units (`rem`)** → if a user changes their default font size, everything scales.
* **Media query** – only a tiny tweak for ultra‑small screens; you can add more breakpoints later if you wish.

---

## 4️⃣  JavaScript – make the app interactive

Open **`script.js`** and paste:

```js
// Grab the elements we need once the DOM is ready
document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
  const form   = document.getElementById('echoForm');
  const input  = document.getElementById('userInput');
  const output = document.getElementById('output');

  // When the form is submitted (button click or Enter key)
  form.addEventListener('submit', (e) => {
    e.preventDefault();               // stop page reload

    const text = input.value.trim();   // remove leading/trailing spaces

    // If the field is empty (shouldn't happen because of "required")
    if (!text) {
      output.textContent = '🤔 Please type something!';
      return;
    }

    // Show the text in the output area
    output.textContent = text;

    // Optional: clear the input and focus it again for fast typing
    input.value = '';
    input.focus();
  });
});
```

### What the script does

| Step | Explanation |
|------|-------------|
| `DOMContentLoaded` | Guarantees the HTML elements exist before we query them. |
| `form.addEventListener('submit', …)` | Handles both the button click **and** pressing **Enter** while the input is focused. |
| `e.preventDefault()` | Stops the default form submission (which would reload the page). |
| `input.value.trim()` | Gets the typed text and removes extra whitespace. |
| `output.textContent = text` | Safely injects the text (no HTML parsing, so no XSS risk). |
| `input.value = ''` + `input.focus()` | Clears the field and puts the cursor back for the next entry – a nice UX touch. |

---

## 5️⃣  Test it locally

1. **Open `index.html` in a browser** – you should see a centered card with a heading, an input, a button, and an empty gray box below.
2. Type something, hit **Show it!** – the text appears in the gray box.
3. Resize the browser window or open the page on a phone. The card should shrink/expand gracefully, and the input/button stay full‑width.

> **Quick tip:** In VS Code install the **Live Server** extension, right‑click `index.html` → *Open with Live Server*. It auto‑reloads when you save a file.

---

## 6️⃣  Going a step further (optional)

| Feature | How to add it (one‑liner) |
|---------|--------------------------|
| **Enter key works** | Already handled because we listen to the form’s `submit` event. |
| **Dark mode** | Add `@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) { … }` in `style.css` and swap background/foreground colors. |
| **Copy to clipboard button** | In JS, after setting `output.textContent`, call `navigator.clipboard.writeText(text)`. |
| **Transition for the output** | Add `transition: background .3s, color .3s;` to `#output` and toggle a class when new text appears. |
| **Deploy** | Push the folder to GitHub and enable **GitHub Pages**, or drop the three files on Netlify/ Vercel (free). |

---

## 7️⃣  Recap – What you now have

```
simple-echo-app/
│
├─ index.html   ← structure, accessibility, meta tags
├─ style.css    ← responsive layout (flexbox, max‑width, media query)
└─ script.js    ← vanilla JS: form handling, DOM update
```

* **Responsive** – thanks to the viewport meta tag, flexible widths, and a tiny media query.
* **Accessible** – proper `<label>`, `aria-live`, and semantic HTML.
* **Maintainable** – HTML, CSS, and JS are each in their own file; you can expand any part without touching the others.

You now have a solid foundation for any small web‑app you want to build next. Happy coding! 🚀

i-dont-need-no-stinking-phone-1:
Below is a quick‑reference guide that covers the most common ways to enumerate **all** files in a directory (and optionally its sub‑directories) across a few popular environments.  
Pick the snippet that matches the language or tool you’re using, then tweak the options (filters, recursion depth, hidden‑file handling, etc.) to fit your exact needs.

---

## 1. Command‑line / Shell

| Tool | Command | What it does | Typical use‑case |
|------|---------|--------------|-----------------|
| **`ls`** | `ls -A /path/to/dir` | Lists non‑dot‑files (but not recursively). | Quick glance, human‑readable output. |
| **`find`** | `find /path/to/dir -type f` | Recursively walks the tree and prints **every** regular file (including hidden ones). | Scripts, pipelines, large trees. |
| **`tree`** | `tree -if --noreport /path/to/dir` | Prints a flat list (`-i`) of all files with full paths (`-f`). | Visual inspection, easy copy‑paste. |
| **`fd`** (modern replacement for `find`) | `fd --type f . /path/to/dir` | Fast, colour‑aware, respects `.gitignore` by default. | Development environments, large repos. |
| **PowerShell** | `Get-ChildItem -Path 'C:\path\to\dir' -File -Recurse` | Returns .NET `FileInfo` objects, easy to pipe further. | Windows automation, object‑oriented pipelines. |

**Tip:** If you only need the *names* (no directories) and you’re on a POSIX system, `find … -type f -printf '%P\n'` prints paths **relative** to the starting directory, which is often what scripts need.

---

## 2. Python

Python’s standard library gives you three idiomatic options, each with its own performance characteristics.

| API | Example | Recursion? | When to use |
|-----|---------|------------|-------------|
| `os.listdir()` | ```python\nimport os\nfiles = [os.path.join(dirpath, f) for f in os.listdir(dirpath) if os.path.isfile(os.path.join(dirpath, f))]\n``` | **No** (only top‑level) | Simple, legacy code, need a plain list of names. |
| `os.scandir()` (Python 3.5+) | ```python\nimport os\nwith os.scandir(dirpath) as it:\n    files = [entry.path for entry in it if entry.is_file()]\n``` | **No** (top‑level) | Faster than `listdir` because it yields `DirEntry` objects that already contain stat info. |
| `pathlib.Path.rglob()` | ```python\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfiles = list(Path(dirpath).rglob('*'))  # includes dirs → filter if needed\nfiles = [p for p in files if p.is_file()]\n``` | **Yes** (recursive) | Modern, object‑oriented, works nicely with `Path` methods. |
| `os.walk()` | ```python\nimport os\nfiles = []\nfor root, _, filenames in os.walk(dirpath):\n    for name in filenames:\n        files.append(os.path.join(root, name))\n``` | **Yes** (recursive) | Full control over traversal (e.g., prune sub‑dirs, follow symlinks). |
| `glob.glob()` | ```python\nimport glob, os\nfiles = [f for f in glob.glob(os.path.join(dirpath, '**'), recursive=True) if os.path.isfile(f)]\n``` | **Yes** (recursive) | Pattern‑based matching (e.g., `*.txt`). |

**Performance note:** For huge directories, `os.scandir` (or `Path.iterdir`) is the fastest because it avoids an extra `stat` call per entry. If you need recursion, `os.walk` internally uses `os.scandir` starting with Python 3.5, so it’s also efficient.

**Hidden files:** In POSIX, files whose names start with `.` are *not* special; they’ll appear automatically. If you want to *exclude* them, filter with `if not name.startswith('.')`.

---

## 3. Java (JDK 8+)

```java
import java.nio.file.*;
import java.util.stream.*;
import java.io.IOException;

public class ListFiles {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {
        Path start = Paths.get("/path/to/dir");

        // 1️⃣ Non‑recursive, just files in the top directory
        try (Stream<Path> stream = Files.list(start)) {
            List<Path> files = stream
                .filter(Files::isRegularFile)
                .collect(Collectors.toList());
        }

        // 2️⃣ Recursive (depth‑first) – all files under start
        try (Stream<Path> walk = Files.walk(start)) {
            List<Path> allFiles = walk
                .filter(Files::isRegularFile)
                .collect(Collectors.toList());
        }

        // 3️⃣ Using a glob pattern (e.g., only *.txt)
        try (DirectoryStream<Path> ds = Files.newDirectoryStream(start, "*.txt")) {
            for (Path p : ds) {
                System.out.println(p);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

* `Files.list` → shallow, returns a `Stream<Path>` (lazy, closeable).  
* `Files.walk` → deep, can limit depth with `Files.walk(start, maxDepth)`.  
* `DirectoryStream` → pattern‑based, efficient for a single directory.

---

## 4. C# / .NET

```csharp
using System;
using System.IO;

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        string dir = @"C:\path\to\dir";

        // 1️⃣ Top‑level only
        string[] topFiles = Directory.GetFiles(dir);
        // or with a search pattern:
        // string[] topTxt = Directory.GetFiles(dir, "*.txt");

        // 2️⃣ Recursive
        string[] allFiles = Directory.GetFiles(dir, "*", SearchOption.AllDirectories);

        // 3️⃣ Using LINQ for extra filtering
        var txtFiles = Directory.EnumerateFiles(dir, "*", SearchOption.AllDirectories)
                               .Where(f => f.EndsWith(".txt", StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase));
    }
}
```

* `Directory.GetFiles` returns a **string[]** (eagerly allocated).  
* `Directory.EnumerateFiles` returns an **IEnumerable<string>** that streams results, which is more memory‑friendly for huge trees.  
* `SearchOption.AllDirectories` triggers recursion; `TopDirectoryOnly` disables it.

---

## 5. JavaScript / Node.js

```js
const fs = require('fs').promises;
const path = require('path');

// Async recursive walk (modern Node ≥10)
async function walk(dir) {
  let entries = await fs.readdir(dir, { withFileTypes: true });
  const files = await Promise.all(entries.map(async entry => {
    const fullPath = path.resolve(dir, entry.name);
    if (entry.isDirectory()) return walk(fullPath);
    if (entry.isFile()) return fullPath;
    return []; // ignore symlinks, sockets, etc.
  }));
  return files.flat();
}

// Usage
walk('/path/to/dir')
  .then(files => console.log(files))
  .catch(err => console.error(err));
```

* `fs.readdir(..., { withFileTypes: true })` gives you `Dirent` objects, letting you test `isFile()` / `isDirectory()` without extra `stat` calls.  
* For a **non‑recursive** list, just drop the `if (entry.isDirectory())` branch and collect the files directly.

---

## 6. Bash / POSIX Shell (pure script)

```bash
#!/usr/bin/env bash
DIR="/path/to/dir"

# 1️⃣ Non‑recursive, plain names (including hidden)
printf '%s\n' "$DIR"/* "$DIR"/.* 2>/dev/null | while IFS= read -r f; do
    [ -f "$f" ] && echo "$f"
done

# 2️⃣ Recursive, one‑per‑line, full paths
find "$DIR" -type f -print
```

* The `printf`/`while` loop avoids the pitfalls of `ls` parsing (e.g., spaces, newlines).  
* `find` is the workhorse for any serious recursion; you can add `-maxdepth N` to limit depth, `-name '*.txt'` to filter, etc.

---

## 7. “Best practice” checklist

| Concern | Recommended approach |
|---------|----------------------|
| **Performance on huge trees** | Use APIs that stream results (`Directory.EnumerateFiles`, `os.scandir`, `Files.walk`, `find` with `-print0` + `xargs -0`). |
| **Memory usage** | Prefer lazy iterators (`yield`, streams, generators) over eager `list`/`array` allocations. |
| **Cross‑platform path handling** | Use language‑specific path abstractions (`Path` in Python, `Path` in Java, `Path` in .NET, `path` module in Node). |
| **Hidden files** | POSIX treats `.`‑prefixed names as ordinary files; filter them manually if you don’t want them. Windows has the “hidden” attribute – most APIs expose it via extra checks (`FileAttributes.Hidden`). |
| **Symlinks / circular references** | Decide whether to follow them. In most shells, `find -L` follows symlinks (risking loops). In Python, `os.walk(..., followlinks=True)` does the same. Guard against infinite recursion by tracking visited inodes or using `-maxdepth`. |
| **Filtering by extension / pattern** | Use glob‑style APIs (`glob`, `Directory.GetFiles(..., "*.txt")`, `PathMatcher` in Java, `fd`/`rg` on the command line). |
| **Error handling** | Expect permission errors, broken symlinks, etc. Most high‑level APIs throw/return exceptions; catch them and decide whether to abort or skip. |
| **Unicode / special characters** | Use APIs that return Unicode strings (all modern languages do). Avoid parsing raw `ls` output; let the language/library give you the filenames directly. |

---

## TL;DR – One‑liner per environment

| Environment | One‑liner (recursive) |
|------------|-----------------------|
| **Bash** | `find /path/to/dir -type f` |
| **PowerShell** | `Get-ChildItem -Path 'C:\path\to\dir' -File -Recurse` |
| **Python** | `list(Path('/path/to/dir').rglob('*'))` (filter with `.is_file()`) |
| **Java** | `Files.walk(Paths.get("/path/to/dir")).filter(Files::isRegularFile).collect(Collectors.toList())` |
| **C#** | `Directory.EnumerateFiles(dir, "*", SearchOption.AllDirectories)` |
| **Node.js** | `await walk('/path/to/dir')` (see snippet above) |

Pick the snippet that matches your stack, adjust the filter (e.g., `*.txt`, `!entry.isHidden()`, etc.), and you’ll have a reliable, performant list of **all** files in the target directory. Happy coding!

motivation-mix-1:
**Solution Explanation**

For every test case we are given a list of `N` integers.
We have to output the same numbers in non‑decreasing order.

The constraints are small (`N ≤ 1000`), therefore any `O(N log N)` sorting
algorithm is more than fast enough.  
We will simply use the built‑in `Array.sort` method of JavaScript, which
implements a fast quick‑/merge‑sort hybrid.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### Algorithm
For each test case  

1. read `N` (the number of elements) – it is not needed afterwards,
   only the following line matters;
2. read the line containing the `N` integers;
3. convert the strings to numbers;
4. sort the array with `arr.sort((a,b)=>a-b)`;
5. output the sorted numbers separated by a single space.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### Correctness Proof  

We prove that the algorithm outputs the required sorted sequence for each
test case.

*Lemma 1*  
`Array.sort((a,b)=>a-b)` returns the elements of the input array in
non‑decreasing order.

*Proof.*  
The comparison function returns a negative value exactly when `a < b`,
zero when `a == b`, and a positive value when `a > b`.  
According to the ECMAScript specification, `Array.prototype.sort`
rearranges the elements so that for any two consecutive elements
`x[i]` and `x[i+1]` the comparison function does **not** return a
positive value, i.e. `x[i] ≤ x[i+1]`. Hence the resulting array is
non‑decreasing. ∎



*Lemma 2*  
The algorithm outputs exactly the multiset of numbers that appear in the
input line for the test case.

*Proof.*  
Step 3 reads every token of the line and converts it to a number,
producing an array `A` that contains each input integer exactly once.
Step 4 only permutes the elements of `A` (no element is added or removed).
Step 5 prints the elements of the permuted array, therefore the output
contains precisely the same numbers as the input. ∎



*Theorem*  
For each test case the algorithm prints the given numbers in
non‑decreasing order.

*Proof.*  
By Lemma 2 the algorithm prints exactly the input numbers.
By Lemma 1 the order after step 4 is non‑decreasing.
Consequently the printed line satisfies the required specification. ∎



--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### Complexity Analysis  

For a test case with `N` numbers:

* reading and converting the numbers: `O(N)`;
* sorting: `O(N log N)`;
* printing: `O(N)`.

Thus the total time is `O(N log N)` and the additional memory used is
`O(N)` for the array of numbers. With `N ≤ 1000` this easily fits the
limits.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### Reference Implementation  (ECMAScript 2021)

```javascript
//  Sorting Numbers – ECMAScript 2021
//  -------------------------------------------------------------

'use strict';
const fs = require('fs');

// read whole input, split by any whitespace
const data = fs.readFileSync(0, 'utf8').trim().split(/\s+/);
let pos = 0;

const T = Number(data[pos++]);          // number of test cases
let output = [];

for (let tc = 0; tc < T; ++tc) {
    const N = Number(data[pos++]);      // size of the array (not needed later)

    // read the N numbers
    const arr = new Array(N);
    for (let i = 0; i < N; ++i) {
        arr[i] = Number(data[pos++]);
    }

    // sort in non‑decreasing order
    arr.sort((a, b) => a - b);

    // prepare line for this test case
    output.push(arr.join(' '));
}

// write all results
console.log(output.join('\n'));
```

The program follows exactly the algorithm proven correct above and
conforms to the ECMAScript 2021 standard.
